# Daily news passages, 12 Nov 2026 (English). Written in our own words from the day's headlines.
EN = {
"E": [
("Sachin Gives His Bats to a Museum", "Sachin Tendulkar is going to give twenty six of his bats to a cricket museum in Mumbai. The museum is run by the Mumbai Cricket Association. Some of these bats were used when he scored centuries for India. He will also give his helmet, a jersey and some of his own notes. Young fans will now be able to see these things with their own eyes. A bat may look like a simple piece of wood, but it tells a long story. It speaks of early morning practice, of hard work in the heat and of the joy of a big win. Sachin started playing for India when he was only sixteen. He played for more than twenty years and made many records. Even today children in small towns try to bat like him. A museum keeps such memories safe for the next generation. When students visit it, they learn that great success comes from years of steady effort and not from luck alone. Let us hope that more players share their treasures in this way."),
("Rain and Snow Alert for Kashmir", "The weather office has said that rain and snow are likely in the hills of the north on the twelfth and thirteenth of November. Parts of the Kashmir valley may get heavy snow. There may also be thunder and strong winds of up to sixty kilometres per hour in Jammu and Kashmir and Ladakh. The change is due to a western disturbance, which is a wind system that comes from the west and brings moisture with it. People in the hills have been asked to stay alert. Travellers should check the road report before they leave home, because high roads may be closed for some time. Farmers should keep their crops and animals in a safe place. Snow is good news for the hotels, as many tourists come to see it, but it can also cause trouble for the local people. In the plains the nights will now become colder. It is a good time to keep warm clothes ready and to take extra care of small children and old people at home."),
("Hybrid Classes in Noida", "Because of the toxic air, schools in Noida and Greater Noida have been told to run hybrid classes for small children up to Class Five. Hybrid means that classes will run in school and online at the same time. Parents can choose whether to send their child to school or to let the child study from home. The order has come into force at once. Doctors say that small children breathe faster than adults, so dirty air harms them more. Coughing, sore eyes and a heavy chest are common in these days. Online classes keep children safe at home, but they also have some problems. Not every home has a good phone or a fast net connection. Some children find it hard to sit in front of a screen for long hours. Teachers are trying to keep lessons short and lively. Parents can help by giving children a quiet corner to study and by checking their homework every day. Clean air is the right of every child, and all of us must work to bring it back."),
("A Ten Year Old Diver Makes History", "Ira, a girl who is only ten years old, has become the youngest scuba diver in the country. She made six world records while diving near Shaheed Dweep in the Andaman Islands. Scuba diving means swimming deep under the sea with a tank of air on the back. It needs training, courage and a calm mind. Divers must learn how to breathe slowly, how to check their tools and how to come up to the surface with care. The sea near the Andaman Islands is clear and blue. Under the water there are colourful fish, corals and many other living things. Ira is now a role model for many children, and especially for girls. Her story shows that age is not a wall when there is hard work and the right guidance. Her parents and her trainers also deserve praise for their support. We should also remember that the sea must be kept clean, so that divers and fish can both enjoy it for many years to come."),
("Badrinath Prepares for Winter", "The process of closing the doors of the Badrinath temple for the winter has begun. Badrinath is one of the four holy places of the Char Dham Yatra in Uttarakhand. It stands high in the Himalayas on the bank of the Alaknanda river. Every year the temple stays open from about April or May till November. When winter comes, heavy snow covers the area and the road becomes unsafe. So the doors are closed with special prayers, and worship continues at a lower place during the cold months. Lakhs of pilgrims visit the temple every season. They come from every part of the country and also from other countries. The local people earn their living from this yatra. They run hotels, shops and taxis, and many work as guides. When the doors close, many of them move to lower towns for the winter. The government also uses this time to repair roads and buildings. In spring the doors open again, and the hills are full of life once more."),
("Hanuman Chalisa in the UK Parliament", "A Diwali event was held at the House of Lords in London, which is a part of the Parliament of the United Kingdom. At the event, the Hanuman Chalisa was recited with live tabla and harmonium. British members of parliament, people of the Indian community and many other guests were present. The hall was full of lamps and flowers. Many guests said that they enjoyed the music and the warm feeling of the festival. Lakhs of people of Indian origin live in the United Kingdom. They work as doctors, teachers, traders and engineers, and many of them are also in public life. Such events help them to stay close to their roots and to share their culture with their neighbours. Festivals like Diwali carry a simple message. They tell us that light wins over darkness and good wins over evil. When people of different faiths come together to celebrate, they learn more about each other. This builds trust and friendship between communities and between countries."),
("Chhath Markets Get Busy", "The markets of Patna are full of people buying things for Chhath Puja. Shops are selling baskets made of bamboo, clay lamps, sugarcane, coconuts, bananas and many other items needed for the puja. Traders say that prices are up to twenty five per cent higher than last year. Even so, people are buying with joy, because Chhath is one of the biggest festivals of Bihar and eastern Uttar Pradesh. During Chhath, people pray to the Sun God. They fast for a long time and stand in the water of a river or a pond to offer prayers to the setting sun and then to the rising sun. Families come together, and those who live far away travel home for the festival. Ghats are cleaned and decorated with lights. Volunteers help old people and children reach the water safely. The festival teaches discipline, patience and respect for nature. It also reminds us to keep our rivers and ponds clean, because they are an important part of our life."),
("Concern Over Ganga Water at Danapur", "Concern has been raised about dirty water of the Ganga at the ghats of Danapur near Patna. Thousands of people will offer prayers there during Chhath Puja. They will stand in the water for a long time. If the water is not clean, it may cause skin and stomach problems. The Ganga is a holy river for crores of people. It also gives water for drinking and farming to many cities and villages. But waste from homes, drains and factories often reaches the river. Plastic, flowers wrapped in polythene and old clothes are also thrown into it. The government has started many plans to clean the river. Plants have been set up to treat dirty water before it goes into the Ganga. But the work cannot succeed without the help of the people. We should not throw waste into the river. We should use dustbins at the ghats and follow the rules. A clean river is the best gift that we can give to the next generation."),
("A Mother Becomes an Officer at Forty", "A woman from Kondli Bangar has been selected as an officer through the Uttar Pradesh PCS examination at the age of forty. She cleared the exam eighteen years after her marriage. For many years she looked after her home and her family. Then she decided to follow her old dream of serving the public. Studying again after a long gap is not easy. She had to manage her home, her children and her books at the same time. She often studied late at night or early in the morning. Many people told her that it was too late to start again. But she did not give up. She made a plan, studied a little every day and kept trying. Her family also stood by her. Her success has given hope to many women who think that their chance has gone. Her story tells us that age is only a number. With a clear goal, steady work and support at home, a person can reach the top at any stage of life."),
("India Meet Maldives in the SAFF Championship", "India are playing the Maldives in a match of the SAFF Championship 2026. The SAFF Championship is a football tournament for the countries of South Asia. Teams such as India, Pakistan, Bangladesh, Nepal, Bhutan, Sri Lanka and the Maldives take part in it. India have won this title many times in the past and are among the strongest teams of the region. Fans can watch the match free on some platforms. Football is the most popular game in the world, and it is growing fast in India too. Many states such as Kerala, West Bengal, Goa and the states of the North East have a long love for the game. Today more young players are joining football academies in other states as well. To do well, a team needs fitness, speed and good team work. Every player must pass the ball well and run without stopping for ninety minutes. Let us cheer for our team and hope that football gets the same love as cricket in our country."),
],
"M": [
("Cabinet Clears Credit Fund and Metro Link", "The Union Cabinet has approved a credit guarantee fund of Rs 5,000 crore under the Mudra scheme. The Cabinet also cleared four other decisions, including the extension of the metro from Noida to Greater Noida, a fertiliser subsidy for the rabi season and the setting up of a new National Museum. The Information and Broadcasting Minister, Ashwini Vaishnaw, briefed the media after the meeting. A credit guarantee fund gives comfort to banks. If a small borrower is unable to repay a loan, a part of the loss is covered by the fund. As a result, banks become more willing to lend to small shopkeepers, artisans and new entrepreneurs who have no property to offer as security. The metro extension will help thousands of office workers and students who travel between the two cities every day. The fertiliser subsidy will keep the price of key inputs low for farmers at the time of sowing. Together, these decisions touch the lives of traders, commuters and farmers alike."),
("A Big Trade Before IPL 2027", "Ahead of IPL 2027, Hardik Pandya has moved from Mumbai Indians to Punjab Kings. His fee will now be Rs 18 crore, up from Rs 16.35 crore. In the same deal, the fast bowler Arshdeep Singh joins Mumbai Indians, also for Rs 18 crore. Such a deal is called a trade. It allows two teams to exchange players before the auction, so that each side can fill a gap in its squad. Hardik is an all-rounder who can bat, bowl and field well. Punjab Kings hope that he will add strength to their middle order and help them in tight finishes. Arshdeep is a left-arm bowler who is known for his skill in the last overs of an innings. Mumbai Indians will want him to lead their pace attack. Fans are already discussing the deal on social media. Some feel that both teams have gained, while others believe that one side has made a better bargain. The real answer will come only when the season begins and the players take the field."),
("Hijack Note Found on Flight", "A flight of Air India from Kolkata to Mumbai received a hijack threat after a paper note was found in a toilet of the aircraft. The pilot at once informed air traffic control. Security agencies then stopped the plane from taking off and checked it thoroughly. Passengers were taken off the aircraft and their bags were checked again. Officials said that such threats are always treated as serious, even when they later turn out to be false. Every airport follows a fixed plan for these cases. The aircraft is moved to a safe area, bomb squads search every corner and the passengers are kept under watch until the check is over. These steps cause delay and trouble for travellers, but safety comes first. Making a false threat is a crime under the law, and the guilty person can be sent to jail. Passengers can also help by reporting any bag or note that looks strange. Alert travellers and quick action by the staff keep air travel safe for everyone."),
("1,167 Posts of Sub-Inspector in Central Forces", "Recruitment is open for 1,167 posts of Sub-Inspector in five central forces, including the Border Security Force. These forces guard our borders and help the police during elections, riots and natural disasters. A Sub-Inspector leads a small team of jawans and plays an important role in day-to-day work. However, not every candidate is eligible to apply. Candidates must read the notice carefully to check the rules about age, education, height and chest measurement. Some posts may need a degree, while others may need a technical skill. The selection usually includes a written test, a physical test, a medical check-up and a review of documents. Those who wish to apply should fill the form on the official website before the last date and should keep a copy of it. They should begin preparing at once. Daily running, a balanced diet and good sleep will help in the physical test. For the written test, they should revise general knowledge, reasoning, mathematics and language every day."),
("Results of the Screening Test for Doctors", "According to a report, about seventy seven per cent of medical graduates who studied abroad could not pass the screening test held in India. However, their scores in October were about ten percentage points better than in June. Every year, thousands of Indian students go to countries such as Russia, Ukraine, China, Kyrgyzstan and the Philippines to study medicine, because seats in India are limited and private colleges are costly. When they return, they must clear a screening test before they can practise as doctors in India. The test checks whether their knowledge meets Indian standards. Experts say that many of these students have little practical training in hospitals and that the language of teaching abroad is also a problem. The improvement in October is a good sign. It shows that students are preparing better and that coaching for the test has improved. In the long run, the country needs more medical seats at a fair cost, so that students do not have to leave home to become doctors."),
("World Bank Praises Water Management", "A senior official of the World Bank has praised India for its work in managing water. Over the past few years, the country has run large schemes to bring tap water to every rural home, to save rain water and to recharge the ground water. Many villages now have pipes, tanks and ponds that were not there earlier. Women and girls who once walked for hours to fetch water can now use that time for study or work. At the same time, an opinion piece in a leading newspaper says that many challenges still remain. In several areas the level of ground water is falling every year. Some pipes run dry in summer, and in other places the water is not safe to drink. Farming uses most of our water, and crops such as rice and sugarcane need a lot of it. Experts suggest drip irrigation, better pricing and the repair of old ponds. Water is a limited gift of nature, so every home, farm and factory must learn to use it with care."),
("Delhi's Air Hits a Three Year Low", "The air quality in Delhi has reached its worst level in three years, according to reports. The Air Quality Index stayed in the very poor and severe range at many stations in the city. Doctors have advised people, especially children, old people and those with asthma, to avoid going out in the morning and late evening. Smoke from vehicles, dust from building sites, the burning of waste and smoke from crop stubble in nearby states are the main causes. In winter the cold and still air keeps these pollutants close to the ground. In other news from the capital, the New Delhi Municipal Council has been placed second in the national cleanliness survey. This shows that a clean city and clean air are two different tasks. Streets can be swept every day, but air can be cleaned only when the sources of pollution are cut down. Both need firm rules, modern machines and, above all, the help of the people who live in the city."),
("Spain Holds a General Strike", "Spain has held its first general strike in fourteen years. In a general strike, workers in many different sectors stop work on the same day to press their demands. Trains, buses, factories, shops and offices were affected in several cities. Large rallies were held in Madrid, Barcelona and other places. Trade unions said that people were unhappy about the high cost of living, the price of housing and the level of wages. Some groups also raised other social and political demands. The government said that it was ready to talk and that it was already working on some of these issues. A strike is a legal right of workers in a democracy, but it also causes loss to the economy and trouble for ordinary people. That is why both sides usually try to settle such disputes through dialogue. The events in Spain show that rising prices are a worry not only in developing countries but also in rich nations of Europe."),
("New Test Schedule to be Proposed in Bali", "The International Cricket Council is expected to discuss the match-ups for the World Test Championship from 2027 to 2031 at a meeting in Bali. The World Test Championship was started to give more meaning to Test cricket, the longest form of the game. In this system, teams earn points for each Test series they play over two years, and the top two teams meet in a final. The new plan will decide which countries play against each other and how many matches each series will have. Cricket boards want a fair schedule, but they also want series that bring large crowds and good television income. Smaller boards often complain that they get fewer matches against big teams such as India, England and Australia. Players also want enough rest between tours. Test cricket tests the skill, patience and fitness of a player like no other format. A balanced schedule can keep it alive and exciting in the age of short and fast cricket."),
("Second Day of Russia-Ukraine Talks", "Russia and Ukraine held a second day of talks in Abu Dhabi, with the help of the United States. The aim of the talks is to find a way to end the war that began in February 2022. Officials did not share many details, but they said that the discussions were serious. The United Arab Emirates has hosted such meetings because it has good relations with both sides. The war has caused great loss of life and property. Millions of people have left their homes, and many cities have been damaged. It has also affected the whole world. The prices of wheat, cooking oil, fertiliser and fuel rose sharply, and poor countries suffered the most. India has always said that dialogue and diplomacy are the only way to solve the conflict. A lasting peace will not come in a day. It will need trust, patience and firm guarantees. Still, every round of talks is a small step towards that goal."),
],
"H": [
("Why a Credit Guarantee Fund Matters", "The Union Cabinet's approval of a Rs 5,000 crore credit guarantee fund under the Pradhan Mantri Mudra Yojana deserves closer attention. Launched in April 2015, the scheme provides collateral-free loans to non-corporate, non-farm micro enterprises in three broad categories - Shishu, Kishore and Tarun - depending on the size of the loan. A guarantee fund does not lend money directly; instead, it promises to compensate the lending institution for a specified portion of the loss if a borrower defaults. Consequently, banks and non-banking finance companies become more willing to extend credit to first-generation entrepreneurs, street vendors and artisans who possess neither property nor a formal credit history. The design, however, requires a delicate balance. If the guarantee is too generous, lenders may become careless in appraisal; if it is too restrictive, the fund remains unused. Experts therefore recommend transparent claim procedures, periodic audits and the use of digital transaction data to assess creditworthiness. Properly managed, such a fund can convert small savings and skills into sustainable livelihoods."),
("Lessons from London's Great Smog", "As Delhi struggles with its worst air in three years, commentators have compared the situation with the Great Smog of London. In December 1952, a thick layer of smoke and fog covered the British capital for nearly five days; cold, windless weather trapped pollutants from coal fires, factories and vehicles close to the ground. Visibility fell to a few metres, transport came to a halt and thousands of people died from breathing problems. The tragedy forced the government to act, and the Clean Air Act of 1956 restricted the burning of coal in cities and encouraged cleaner fuels. Delhi's problem, of course, is more complex. Its pollution comes from many sources - vehicles, construction dust, industries, waste burning and crop residue from neighbouring states - and it crosses administrative boundaries. Nevertheless, London's experience offers a clear lesson: improvement came only when the law was strict, enforcement was consistent and citizens accepted changes in their daily habits. Short-term emergency measures can reduce the peak, but only sustained, region-wide action can solve the problem."),
("A Tough Test in Israel's Knesset", "Former military chief Gadi Eisenkot has been sworn in as a member of Israel's new Knesset, the country's 120-member parliament, and has introduced bills intended to reverse several policies of Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu. According to reports, the proposals include an independent inquiry into the attack of 7 October 2023 and the restoration of powers of the judiciary that had been curtailed. Reports also state that he has six weeks to demonstrate that he commands a majority, a task that analysts consider difficult. Israel follows a system of proportional representation; because no single party usually wins 61 seats on its own, governments are formed through coalitions of several parties with different priorities. Such arrangements give small parties considerable bargaining power and often make governments unstable. The debate over the powers of the judiciary has divided Israeli society for years, provoking large protests and concern among allies. For students of international affairs, the episode illustrates how electoral systems shape political stability, and why coalition building demands compromise, patience and clear rules of procedure."),
("Political Funding and Transparency", "A report based on disclosures made to the Election Commission of India shows that donations received by the Congress have fallen by about 75 per cent, and that electoral trusts have also reduced their contributions. Electoral trusts are non-profit companies that collect money from corporate donors and distribute it to political parties; they must report the names of donors and recipients every year. After the Supreme Court struck down the electoral bond scheme in February 2024, observers expected a shift in the way parties raise funds, and annual statements are now being studied closely. Money is necessary for democratic politics: parties must pay for offices, staff, travel, publicity and campaigns. The central concern, however, is transparency. Citizens have a right to know who funds political parties, because large donations may create an expectation of favours. Experts have suggested several reforms - limits on cash donations, mandatory online disclosure within a fixed time, independent audits and partial state funding of elections. Whatever the method, public confidence depends on rules that are clear, uniform and strictly enforced."),
("A New National Museum for the Nation", "Among the decisions approved by the Union Cabinet is the creation of a new National Museum. Museums are far more than buildings that store old objects; they are institutions that preserve memory, support research and shape national identity. India's existing National Museum in New Delhi, opened in 1949, holds a vast collection that spans the Harappan civilisation, Mauryan and Gupta sculpture, Mughal miniatures, manuscripts, coins and arms. A modern museum, however, must do much more than display artefacts behind glass. It requires scientific conservation laboratories, climate-controlled storage, careful documentation and digital catalogues that scholars across the world can consult. It should also tell stories in a manner that engages schoolchildren, using audio guides in several languages, interactive galleries and facilities for persons with disabilities. Equally important is the question of provenance: many objects left the country during colonial rule, and their return requires patient diplomacy and legal effort. If planned with vision, the new museum can become a centre of learning, tourism and cultural diplomacy, and can help every citizen understand the depth and diversity of India's past."),
("The Economics of Fertiliser Subsidy", "The Union Cabinet has approved the fertiliser subsidy for the coming rabi season, when wheat, mustard, gram and other winter crops are sown. India's fertiliser policy has two main parts. Urea is sold at a fixed maximum retail price, and the government pays manufacturers the difference between the cost of production and that price. Phosphatic and potassic fertilisers, such as DAP and MOP, fall under the Nutrient Based Subsidy scheme introduced in 2010, under which a fixed subsidy is paid per kilogram of nutrient - nitrogen, phosphorus, potash and sulphur. Because India imports a large share of these fertilisers or their raw materials, global prices and exchange rates directly affect the subsidy bill. Agricultural scientists have long warned that cheap urea encourages its excessive use, disturbing the ideal ratio of 4:2:1 for nitrogen, phosphorus and potash and harming soil health. Soil Health Cards, neem-coated urea and nano fertilisers have been promoted to improve efficiency. The challenge for policy-makers is to keep inputs affordable for farmers while encouraging balanced and sustainable use of nutrients."),
("Extending the Metro to Greater Noida", "The Cabinet's approval for extending the metro from Noida to Greater Noida reflects the rapid growth of the National Capital Region. Over the past two decades, Greater Noida has emerged as a hub of universities, industries, housing societies and logistics parks; meanwhile, thousands of residents commute daily to offices in Noida and Delhi. A dependable metro link can reduce travel time, cut fuel consumption and lower vehicular emissions - an important consideration in a region where winter air frequently becomes hazardous. Urban planners, however, emphasise that a metro line alone is insufficient. Its success depends upon 'last-mile connectivity': feeder buses, shared electric autorickshaws, safe footpaths, bicycle lanes and adequate parking near stations. Transit-oriented development, which encourages offices, shops and homes within walking distance of stations, can further increase ridership. Financial sustainability is another concern, because fares must remain affordable while the operator recovers its considerable operating costs. Integrated ticketing across metro, bus and rail services, together with careful route planning, can ensure that this public investment delivers lasting benefits to commuters."),
("Reforming the Screening of Foreign Medical Graduates", "The latest results of the screening test for foreign medical graduates once again highlight a long-standing problem. According to reports, nearly 77 per cent of candidates did not qualify, although average scores improved by about ten percentage points compared with the previous session. Indian students who complete an MBBS-equivalent degree abroad must pass this examination, conducted by the National Board of Examinations in Medical Sciences, before they can register with a medical council and practise in India. Critics argue that the low pass rate reflects variable standards in foreign institutions, limited clinical exposure and teaching in unfamiliar languages; others maintain that the test is necessary precisely because patient safety cannot be compromised. The proposed National Exit Test (NExT) aims to create a common licensing examination for graduates of both Indian and foreign colleges, thereby ensuring uniform standards. Meanwhile, the deeper solution lies at home: increasing the number of affordable medical seats, strengthening district hospitals as teaching centres and regulating fees in private colleges. Only then will students not need to travel thousands of kilometres to pursue their ambition of becoming doctors."),
("How Airports Respond to Threats", "The recent incident in which an Air India flight from Kolkata to Mumbai was stopped after a threatening note was discovered in a lavatory shows how seriously the aviation sector treats every warning. In India, the Bureau of Civil Aviation Security (BCAS) lays down security standards, while the Central Industrial Security Force (CISF) protects most major airports. When a threat is received, a Bomb Threat Assessment Committee, comprising officials of the airline, the airport operator, security agencies and local police, quickly decides whether the threat is 'specific' or 'non-specific'. A specific threat triggers the full protocol: the aircraft is moved to an isolation bay, passengers and baggage are screened again and bomb disposal squads search the entire aircraft. Such measures inevitably cause delays and inconvenience; nevertheless, aviation safety depends on treating every warning as genuine until it is disproved. Hoax threats waste valuable resources and are punishable under the law, and amendments have made the penalties stricter. Ultimately, vigilant passengers, trained crew and coordinated agencies together form the most effective defence against such dangers."),
("Learning Through a Pollution Emergency", "The decision to run hybrid classes for children up to Class Five in Noida and Greater Noida reflects a difficult choice between health and learning. When the Air Quality Index rises to 'severe' levels, the Graded Response Action Plan (GRAP), implemented by the Commission for Air Quality Management, allows authorities to restrict construction, regulate vehicles and modify school timings or switch classes online. Younger children are more vulnerable to polluted air because their lungs are still developing and they breathe faster than adults. However, prolonged closure of schools has its own costs. Experience during the pandemic showed that online education widened the gap between families who possess smartphones and stable internet connections and those who do not; moreover, children lost opportunities for play, nutrition through mid-day meals and social development. Educationists therefore recommend flexible arrangements: shorter school hours, indoor activities, air purifiers in classrooms where possible, printed worksheets for students without devices and careful monitoring of attendance. Above all, such measures must remain temporary; the lasting solution is to reduce the sources of pollution so that classrooms remain open and safe."),
],
}
